{"name":"Blue Heeler","hex":"#939cab","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue heeler","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A blue-coated variety of the Australian Cattle Dog breed.","A police officer."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_heeler"}],"entries":[{"title":"Australian Cattle Dog","blocks":[{"text":"The Australian Cattle Dog, or simply Cattle Dog, is a breed of herding dog developed in Australia for droving cattle over long distances across rough terrain. This breed is a medium-sized, short-coated dog that occurs in two main colour forms. It has either red or black hair distributed fairly evenly through a white coat, which gives the appearance of a \"red\" or \"blue\" dog."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/ACD-blue-spud.jpg/330px-ACD-blue-spud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Australian_Cattle_Dog"},{"title":"Herding dog","blocks":[{"text":"A herding dog, also known as a stock dog or working dog, is a type of dog that either has been trained in herding livestock or belongs to one of the breeds that were developed for herding. A dog specifically trained to herd sheep is known as a sheep dog or shepherd dog, and one trained to herd cattle is known as a cattle dog or cow dog."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Kelpie_walking_across_the_backs_of_sheep.jpg/330px-Kelpie_walking_across_the_backs_of_sheep.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Herding_dog"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Cattledog_Little_Joe.JPG/500px-Cattledog_Little_Joe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Cattledog_Little_Joe.JPG/1280px-Cattledog_Little_Joe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Cattledog_Little_Joe.JPG/500px-Cattledog_Little_Joe.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Black mask and tan markings on a blue dog","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACattledog_Little_Joe.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Australian_Cattle_Dog_puppy.jpg/330px-Australian_Cattle_Dog_puppy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Australian_Cattle_Dog_puppy.jpg/330px-Australian_Cattle_Dog_puppy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Red hairs will grow through the puppy's white coat as it matures.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustralian_Cattle_Dog_puppy.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Australian_Catle_Dog_Silverbarn%27s_Paavo.jpg/500px-Australian_Catle_Dog_Silverbarn%27s_Paavo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Australian_Catle_Dog_Silverbarn%27s_Paavo.jpg/1280px-Australian_Catle_Dog_Silverbarn%27s_Paavo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Australian_Catle_Dog_Silverbarn%27s_Paavo.jpg/500px-Australian_Catle_Dog_Silverbarn%27s_Paavo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A Cattle Dog with a single mask and a \"bentley mark\" shows the breed's typical alert expression.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustralian_Catle_Dog_Silverbarn's_Paavo.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Acd_agility.jpg/500px-Acd_agility.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Acd_agility.jpg/500px-Acd_agility.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The breed is well suited for agility trials.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAcd_agility.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Australian_Cattle_Dog_Herding.jpg/330px-Australian_Cattle_Dog_Herding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Australian_Cattle_Dog_Herding.jpg/330px-Australian_Cattle_Dog_Herding.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Australian Cattle Dogs were bred to drive cattle, but are also used to herd sheep.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAustralian_Cattle_Dog_Herding.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/17_year_old_ACD_Ruby.jpg/500px-17_year_old_ACD_Ruby.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https:/